Subject: [PATCH 0017] _elementtree: carry the join flag by pointer arithmetic
Upstream: CPython 3.13.7
Upstream-Archive: https://www.python.org/ftp/python/3.13.7/Python-3.13.7.tgz
Upstream-SHA256: 6c9d80839cfa20024f34d9a6dd31ae2a9cd97ff5e980e969209746037a5153b2
Build-Variants: capstone64 virtual application (musl); native
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

An Element keeps a "join" flag in the low bit of its text and tail
pointers. Upstream sets and clears it through uintptr_t: JOIN_OBJ is
(uintptr_t)p & ~1 and JOIN_SET is (uintptr_t)p | flag. Where a pointer is a
capability, the pointer rebuilt from that integer is untagged, and the first
use of one faults: ET.Element('a') dies in element_init, where
_set_joined_ptr decrements the reference count of JOIN_OBJ(self->text)
(cause 24, measured 2026-10-11 on both virtual images,
results/2026-10-11-same-answers, finding F3). regrtest's --junit-xml builds
its report with ElementTree and dies the same way.

JOIN_OBJ and JOIN_SET become inline functions that subtract and add the flag
on the pointer itself, so the result is derived from the pointer and keeps
its capability. Arithmetic is done only when the flag is set or is being set,
so NULL (element_gc_traverse after a clear) and unflagged pointers pass
through untouched. JOIN_GET still reads the address bit. On a platform where
a pointer is an address, the results are the same values as upstream's.

--- a/Modules/_elementtree.c
+++ b/Modules/_elementtree.c
@@ -55,8 +55,25 @@
    JOIN_OBJ.  see comments in the ElementObject definition for more
    info. */
 #define JOIN_GET(p) ((uintptr_t) (p) & 1)
-#define JOIN_SET(p, flag) ((void*) ((uintptr_t) (JOIN_OBJ(p)) | (flag)))
-#define JOIN_OBJ(p) ((PyObject*) ((uintptr_t) (p) & ~(uintptr_t)1))
+#define JOIN_SET(p, flag) _join_set((PyObject *) (p), (flag))
+#define JOIN_OBJ(p) _join_obj((PyObject *) (p))
+
+/* The flag is added to and subtracted from the pointer, never masked
+   through an integer: a pointer rebuilt from an integer has lost its
+   provenance, and where a pointer is a capability it is no longer one.
+   NULL and unflagged pointers are returned as they are. */
+static inline PyObject *
+_join_obj(PyObject *p)
+{
+    return JOIN_GET(p) ? (PyObject *) ((char *) p - 1) : p;
+}
+
+static inline void *
+_join_set(PyObject *p, int flag)
+{
+    p = _join_obj(p);
+    return flag ? (void *) ((char *) p + 1) : (void *) p;
+}
 
 /* Py_SETREF for a PyObject* that uses a join flag. */
 Py_LOCAL_INLINE(void)
